Method: public boolean offer(E e) { ... return true; // @@@ If the element is not null, then the result is true. }

We need assert that if e != null then \result == true. Since method returns boolean. Also note that method throws NullPointerException if e == null before reaching return. So precondition: e != null => result true. Also if e == null, method throws; but spec only about non-null. So assert: e != null => \result == true.

In JML style: assert e != null => \result == true; Or could be assert (e != null) => \result; Since boolean true is same as \result. But better explicit.

